    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"5 · Roll forward and validate\"\nsubtitle: \"Solve the equation for a year the model has never seen\"\n---\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"stepmeta\"><b>You need</b><span><code>Theta</code>, <code>tr</code>, <code>gram</code> and a fitting method from step 4.</span><b>You get</b><span>predicted biomass trajectories for held-out years, and a table comparing methods.</span></div>\n```\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n## Solving the equation is a running total\n\nThe fitted rule says how fast biomass grows on each day, given that day's weather. Because the library depends only on the weather, there is nothing to integrate numerically: biomass is the starting value plus the running sum of daily growth.\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_0 + \\sum_{s<t} \\Theta(u_s)\\,\\xi\n$$\n\n```r\nsimulate <- function(xi, Theta_y, y0) {\n  inc <- Theta_y[-nrow(Theta_y), , drop = FALSE] %*% t(xi)         # (days-1) x genotype increments\n  sweep(rbind(0, apply(inc, 2, cumsum)), 2, y0, \"+\")               # day x genotype\n}\n```\n\n## One held-out year\n\nFit on all years except 1998, then predict 1998 from its weather alone. `fit_years` chooses the shared terms with the chosen method, then adds a small shrunk deviation for each genotype (step 7).\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nyv    <- match(1998, tr$years)\ntrain <- setdiff(seq_along(tr$years), yv)\nf     <- fit_years(gram, train, fit_ssr, k = 12)\ny0    <- apply(tr$Y[, train, 1], 1, mean)                       # starting biomass: training mean\npred  <- simulate(f$xi, Theta[yv, , ], y0)                      # day x genotype\nf$names[f$support]                                              # the 12 shared terms\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n [1] \"1\"     \"TT\"    \"TT^2\"  \"TT^3\"  \"DL\"    \"DL^2\"  \"DL^3\"  \"RAW\"   \"RAW^2\"\n[10] \"RAD\"   \"RAD^2\" \"RAD^3\"\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ngs <- c(1, 70, 140, 199); rows <- list()\nfor (g in gs) { n <- tr$nend[g, yv] + 1\n  rows[[length(rows) + 1]] <- data.frame(genotype = tr$genotypes[g], das = (1:n) + tr$das0 - 1,\n    APSIM = tr$Y[g, yv, 1:n], SINDy = pred[1:n, g]) }\nd <- do.call(rbind, rows); d <- reshape(d, direction = \"long\", varying = c(\"APSIM\", \"SINDy\"), v.names = \"biomass\", timevar = \"source\", times = c(\"APSIM\", \"SINDy\"), idvar = c(\"genotype\", \"das\"))\nggplot(d, aes(das, biomass, colour = source, linetype = source)) + geom_line(linewidth = 0.9) + facet_wrap(~genotype, nrow = 1) +\n  scale_colour_manual(values = unname(dgxe[c(\"blue\", \"orange\")])) + labs(x = \"days after sowing\", y = \"biomass (t/ha)\", colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) + theme_dgxe()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](05-validate_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){width=1036.8}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Leave-one-year-out cross-validation\n\nRepeat for every year: each year is predicted by a model that never saw it. Seeing only weather forecasts, not APSIM biomass, is the point; this is the honest test.\n\n```r\ncv_blocked <- function(Theta, tr, gram, fitter = fit_ssr, k = 12, lam_mu = 1e-4, lam_g = 0.01, ...) {\n  ny <- length(tr$years); G <- length(tr$genotypes); nd <- dim(Theta)[2]\n  P <- array(NA_real_, c(G, ny, nd)); S_all <- vector(\"list\", ny)\n  for (yv in seq_len(ny)) {\n    train <- setdiff(seq_len(ny), yv); f <- fit_years(gram, train, fitter, k, lam_mu, lam_g, ...)\n    y0 <- apply(tr$Y[, train, 1, drop = FALSE], 1, mean)                        # initial biomass: mean of training years\n    P[, yv, ] <- t(simulate(f$xi, Theta[yv, , ], y0)); S_all[[yv]] <- f$support\n  }\n  list(pred = P, support = S_all)\n}\n```\n\n```r\nevaluate <- function(P, tr, fixed_day = 99) {\n  G <- dim(P)[1]; ny <- dim(P)[2]; se <- 0; cnt <- 0; fin <- numeric(0)\n  for (g in seq_len(G)) for (y in seq_len(ny)) {\n    n <- tr$nend[g, y]; d <- 2:(n + 1)                      # day index 1..n (day 0 is the given start)\n    se <- se + sum((P[g, y, d] - tr$Y[g, y, d])^2); cnt <- cnt + length(d)\n    fin <- c(fin, P[g, y, n + 1] - tr$Y[g, y, n + 1])\n  }\n  c(trajectory_rmse = sqrt(se / cnt), fixed_day_rmse = sqrt(mean((P[, , fixed_day] - tr$Y[, , fixed_day])^2, na.rm = TRUE)),\n    maturity_rmse = sqrt(mean(fin^2)))\n}\n```\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nsystem.time(cv <- cv_blocked(Theta, tr, gram, fit_ssr, k = 12))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n   user  system elapsed \n  3.430   0.004   3.491 \n```\n\n\n:::\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nround(evaluate(cv$pred, tr, fixed_day = 99), 3)       # array index 99 = day 113 after sowing\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\ntrajectory_rmse  fixed_day_rmse   maturity_rmse \n          0.521           0.689           0.747 \n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## A baseline that uses no weather\n\nHow much does the weather add? A simple competitor: predict each genotype's biomass curve by the **average of its own curves in the other years**.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nbase <- array(NA_real_, dim(tr$Y))\nfor (y in seq_along(tr$years)) base[, y, ] <- apply(tr$Y[, -y, , drop = FALSE], c(1, 3), mean, na.rm = TRUE)\nfor (g in 1:dim(base)[1]) for (y in 1:dim(base)[2]) {\n  v <- base[g, y, ]; ok <- which(!is.nan(v)); v[is.nan(v)] <- v[max(ok)]               # after the last other year ends: hold the last mean\n  base[g, y, ] <- v; base[g, y, 1] <- tr$Y[g, y, 1]                                    # same start as the model\n}\nround(evaluate(base, tr, fixed_day = 99), 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\ntrajectory_rmse  fixed_day_rmse   maturity_rmse \n          0.739           0.840           1.423 \n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Swap the fitting method\n\nBecause every method has the signature `fit(A, b, ...)`, comparing them is one loop:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nmethods <- list(OLS = list(fit_ols), ridge = list(fit_ridge, lambda = 0.01),\n                SSR_8 = list(fit_ssr, k = 8), SSR_12 = list(fit_ssr, k = 12), lasso = list(fit_lasso, lambda = 2e-4))\nres <- cached(\"swap_methods.rds\", do.call(rbind, lapply(names(methods), function(nm) {\n  m <- methods[[nm]]; cv <- do.call(cv_blocked, c(list(Theta, tr, gram, m[[1]]), m[-1]))\n  data.frame(method = nm, t(round(evaluate(cv$pred, tr), 3))) })))\nres <- rbind(res, data.frame(method = \"no weather (lookup)\", t(round(evaluate(base, tr), 3))))\nres\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n               method trajectory_rmse fixed_day_rmse maturity_rmse\n1                 OLS           0.516          0.684         0.750\n2               ridge           0.516          0.684         0.750\n3               SSR_8           0.612          0.784         0.884\n4              SSR_12           0.521          0.689         0.747\n5               lasso           0.532          0.718         0.764\n6 no weather (lookup)           0.739          0.840         1.423\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOLS and ridge are best on this small library, and the sparse fits reach the same accuracy with 12 of 13 terms; k = 8 is visibly worse. The lookup baseline is the reference: if a model cannot beat it, the weather is not helping.\n\n## How many terms? A larger library\n\nWith the 13-term library, sparsity buys little: it is already small. Add products of drivers (`pairs = TRUE`, 37 terms) and the picture changes:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nTheta2 <- make_library(drv, pairs = TRUE); gram2 <- gram_stats(Theta2, tr)\nks <- c(6, 8, 12, 16, 20, 30, 37)\nrk <- cached(\"rmse_vs_k_pairs.rds\", data.frame(k = ks, rmse = sapply(ks, function(k) evaluate(cv_blocked(Theta2, tr, gram2, fit_ssr, k = k)$pred, tr)[1])))\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nggplot(rk, aes(k, rmse)) + geom_line(colour = dgxe[[\"blue\"]], linewidth = 0.9) + geom_point(colour = dgxe[[\"blue\"]], size = 2.4) +\n  geom_hline(yintercept = res$trajectory_rmse[res$method == \"OLS\"], colour = dgxe[[\"orange\"]], linetype = 2) +\n  annotate(\"text\", x = 37, y = res$trajectory_rmse[res$method == \"OLS\"], label = \"13-term OLS\", vjust = -0.6, hjust = 1, colour = dgxe[[\"orange\"]], size = 3.4) +\n  labs(x = \"number of terms kept (SSR)\", y = \"held-out trajectory RMSE (t/ha)\") + theme_dgxe()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](05-validate_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-10-1.png){width=1036.8}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {.callout-note title=\"Idea\"}\n**Predicting forward is a running total, and it is judged on years the model never saw.** Error accumulates along the rollout, so this is a stricter test than one-step prediction. A model can fit daily increments well and still drift.\n:::\n\n::: {.callout-tip title=\"Check\"}\nThe model should beat the weather-free lookup, and the held-out error should be larger than the in-sample error. Plot a few trajectories: errors that grow steadily through the season point to a missing driver.\n:::\n\n::: {.callout-important title=\"Pitfall\"}\n**Never validate by randomly holding out days.** Neighbouring days of one year are nearly identical, and a year is the unit of independent weather, so we hold out whole years. The same goes for genotypes of the same year when you ask about new weather.\n:::\n",
